1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,728
Saturday, one in the morning, the stolen phone lights up in the thief's pocket.

2
00:00:04,778 --> 00:00:06,168
It is a push from the bank.

3
00:00:06,218 --> 00:00:09,816
The thief leans in to read it, and finds nothing worth reading.

4
00:00:09,866 --> 00:00:13,920
Why does a message from the bank carry so little, on purpose?

5
00:00:13,970 --> 00:00:23,088
In Lesson 12, TAF sent the codes in the master app, and in Lesson 26, the bank's SMS service sent them in the side apps.

6
00:00:23,138 --> 00:00:30,048
This lesson collects every message the platform sends: which road it takes, whom it may reach, and what it may say.

7
00:00:30,098 --> 00:00:31,920
First, the roads.

8
00:00:31,970 --> 00:00:34,824
The platform has no messaging contract of its own.

9
00:00:34,874 --> 00:00:43,896
Every SMS goes through the SMS service of the bank, and every push goes through the push service, in the Firebase project of the bank.

10
00:00:43,946 --> 00:00:45,192
Codes follow the app.

11
00:00:45,242 --> 00:00:52,944
In the master app, TAF sends the code, and the bank's SMS service sends it only while TAF is down.

12
00:00:52,994 --> 00:00:58,656
In a side app, on a phone or on the web, the bank's SMS service always sends it.

13
00:00:58,706 --> 00:01:02,328
Only the code message carries a code, and a push never does.

14
00:01:02,378 --> 00:01:04,320
Second, the addresses.

15
00:01:04,370 --> 00:01:10,488
An SMS goes only to the number of the registration, or to the verified number of the customer.

16
00:01:10,538 --> 00:01:13,392
A push goes only to the devices of the customer.

17
00:01:13,442 --> 00:01:16,704
Now, how does a message leave without getting lost?

18
00:01:16,754 --> 00:01:18,888
Say Aziz changes his password.

19
00:01:18,938 --> 00:01:27,312
In the same database transaction, the platform writes the new password digest, and a row in the outbox: send password changed.

20
00:01:27,362 --> 00:01:34,440
A relay publishes that row to Kafka, and a worker hands it to the SMS service or the push service.

21
00:01:34,490 --> 00:01:40,152
If the platform crashes in the middle, the row is still there, and the relay sends it later.

22
00:01:40,202 --> 00:01:46,560
And if the change itself fails, there is no row, so there is no message about a change that never happened.

23
00:01:46,610 --> 00:01:51,144
The services send back delivery reports, and the platform records them.

24
00:01:51,194 --> 00:01:58,008
A failed dispatch goes again, as often as the configured retry policy says, and then the platform records it as failed.

25
00:01:58,058 --> 00:01:59,880
Third, what must be sent.

26
00:01:59,930 --> 00:02:04,728
Some messages are security notifications, and the customer cannot switch them off.

27
00:02:04,778 --> 00:02:14,400
A new session sends a push to all his other sessions, and an SMS to his verified number, with the app, the device model, the network address and the time.

28
00:02:14,450 --> 00:02:24,072
A login from a new device, a password change, a locked password, a block, an unblock: each one has its own template, and each one goes out.

29
00:02:24,122 --> 00:02:26,112
Now the rule from Lesson 31.

30
00:02:26,162 --> 00:02:29,424
When Aziz taps this was not me, his number goes on hold.

31
00:02:29,474 --> 00:02:35,736
From then on, every message goes by push only, and no SMS goes to that number while it is on hold.

32
00:02:35,786 --> 00:02:41,736
The reason is simple: the person who holds that SIM card may be the person Aziz is running from.

33
00:02:41,786 --> 00:02:46,320
Fourth, what a message may say, and this is where the thief comes back.

34
00:02:46,370 --> 00:02:50,880
No message contains document data or the personal identification number.

35
00:02:50,930 --> 00:02:56,088
No message has a name other than the first name, and no message has a link that starts an action.

36
00:02:56,138 --> 00:03:00,960
A message about a login may show the device model, the network address and the time.

37
00:03:01,010 --> 00:03:05,208
And a push never contains a code, a text or personal data.

38
00:03:05,258 --> 00:03:08,304
It carries only the type of the message and a reference.

39
00:03:08,354 --> 00:03:13,200
The app gets the words afterwards: it asks the platform for the text with the reference.

40
00:03:13,250 --> 00:03:21,096
Only a device of the customer can get that text, in the customer's language, from the template version that was published when the message left.

41
00:03:21,146 --> 00:03:26,208
So the thief on Saturday night sees, at most, which kind of message the bank sent.

42
00:03:26,258 --> 00:03:32,184
I think this is the most honest design for a phone that can be lost: the push is a doorbell, not a letter.

43
00:03:32,234 --> 00:03:37,440
The push service is outside the bank's perimeter, so it learns nothing about Aziz either.

44
00:03:37,490 --> 00:03:42,648
And when the push service is down, notifications go by SMS only.

45
00:03:42,698 --> 00:03:44,592
Last, the words themselves.

46
00:03:44,642 --> 00:03:47,136
Each template exists in the four languages.

47
00:03:47,186 --> 00:03:52,656
Compliance changes a template with maker-checker, and the platform keeps every version.

48
00:03:52,706 --> 00:03:56,832
The audit event of each message names the version that was sent.

49
00:03:56,882 --> 00:04:07,080
To recap: two roads, the bank's SMS service and the push service, and codes by TAF in the master app and by the bank's SMS in side apps.

50
00:04:07,130 --> 00:04:17,736
An outbox so nothing is lost, security notifications that nobody can switch off, no SMS to a number on hold, and pushes that carry only a type and a reference.

51
00:04:17,786 --> 00:04:21,024
But each of these messages also became an audit event.

52
00:04:21,074 --> 00:04:24,888
Months from now, someone will ask what really happened on Friday night.

53
00:04:24,938 --> 00:04:28,872
How does the bank prove it, and prove that nobody changed the proof?

54
00:04:28,922 --> 00:04:30,744
Next: the audit trail.
